  "requirement": "Marks an agent request that can still accept reusable context before adding node contents.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub struct ContextPhase;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.ContextPhase` is a declaration with no MUST, SHOULD or MAY clause. Its one testable obligation is that the SDK publicly exports an item named `ContextPhase`, shown as a unit struct. The marker's behaviour is covered elsewhere. `context` is defined only on `Ask<..., ContextPhase>`, and `ValidationContext::ask` returns that phase. Those behaviours belong to the refining requirements `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.agent_context` and `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.judge.phases`. `struct[ContextPhase]` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs` carries an {impl} reference to the second one. This subject's tests need not cover them.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` is the marked test. It builds rustdoc for the SDK from the current source tree. It then compares the documented item paths and kinds with the exports listed in the requirements book. It asserts that nothing is missing or unexpected. Removing `ContextPhase`, making it private, or turning it into a different kind of item would fail that assertion. I did not read the helpers `specified_exports` and `documented_exports`. I relied on the test's comments and its assertion messages.\n\nThe check might not notice a change from a unit struct to a struct with fields. The signature `pub struct ContextPhase;` is illustrative, not a stated MUST, so that is not a gap.\n\nI found no untested obligation, and the acknowledgments list is empty.",
